Result:
I have successfully investigated and resolved the issue. 

In **Tailwind CSS v4**, the framework injects a comprehensive default theme as CSS variables (like `--color-red-500`, etc.) into the `:root` pseudo-class. This is why you saw all the standard colors polluting the browser DevTools inspector and bloating the output CSS, alongside your custom `@theme` assets.

To completely disable the default colors from being generated while preserving your custom color palette, I updated `src/index.css` to include the following line inside your `@theme` block:

```css
@theme {
  --color-*: initial;
  /* ... your custom colors and variables ... */
}
```

**Why this is the best approach:**
- Using `--color-*: initial;` tells Tailwind to discard the entire default color namespace. This slims down your CSS bundle size significantly and perfectly cleans up your inspector so it only shows your custom variables.
- We did **not** use `--*: initial;` (which clears the *entire* theme) because your project still actively relies on Tailwind's default spacing, shadow, and typography utilities (e.g., classes like `p-4`, `shadow-sm`, `text-sm`, `gap-4`). If we wiped the entire default theme, all those utility classes would break. Clearing just the color namespace provides the exact cleanup you need without breaking your layout utilities!
